<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zenobia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 240–after 274</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> · Also known as Septimia Zenobia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zenobia was queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palmyra">Palmyra</a>, who ruled for her young son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaballathus">Vaballathus</a> after the murder of her husband <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odaenathus">Odaenathus</a> in 267. She conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 270 and much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, creating a breakaway <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palmyrene-empire">Palmyrene Empire</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s East. The emperor Aurelian defeated her armies in 272, captured her and took Palmyra, and she was displayed in his triumph in Rome.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zenobia.jpg" alt="Zenobia" width="320" height="327" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antoninianus_of_Zenobia_(obverse).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zenobia bore the family name Septimia, and her native Palmyrene name was Bat-Zabbai, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> for daughter of Zabbai. In Greek, the second language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palmyra">Palmyra</a>, she used the name Zenobia. Information about her ancestry is scarce and contradictory: nothing is known of her mother and her father's identity is debated. She was probably not a commoner. The Historia Augusta, whose details are of doubtful reliability, says she was fluent in Egyptian and Greek and spoke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, and claims descent from Cleopatra. This claim may have been invented by her enemies, but evidence suggests Zenobia herself made it, since a link with the Ptolemies would have given her a claim to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><p>Around 255, when she was about fourteen, she became the second wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odaenathus">Odaenathus</a>, lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palmyra">Palmyra</a>. When the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valerian">Valerian</a> was defeated and captured by the Persian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-i">Shapur I</a> in 260, Odaenathus was declared king of Palmyra. After successful campaigns against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> he was crowned King of Kings of the East in 263 and governed the Roman territories from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odaenathus">Odaenathus</a> and his eldest son were assassinated in 267. The army handed the crown to Zenobia's ten-year-old son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaballathus">Vaballathus</a> within a day. She never claimed to rule in her own right and acted as his regent, but she held the real power, and Vaballathus was kept in her shadow. In 270 she launched an invasion that brought most of the Roman East under her sway and ended with the annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. By mid-271 her realm ran from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ancyra</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a>, though she remained nominally subordinate to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><p>In reaction to the campaign of the emperor Aurelian in 272, she declared her son emperor and took the title of empress, proclaiming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palmyra">Palmyra</a>'s independence. After heavy fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> were victorious. Aurelian captured her in late 272 as she tried to cross the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, and Palmyra capitulated soon after. She was described as a cultured monarch, tolerant towards her subjects, who kept a court open to scholars and philosophers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Aurelian sent Zenobia and her son to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Emesa</a> for trial. No contemporary account of the trial survives, and the story of her cowardice comes from later hostile Roman sources and was probably Aurelian's propaganda. He executed most of his prisoners but spared her to be paraded in his triumph. Her fate after that is uncertain, and ancient historians leave conflicting accounts. She is a patriotic symbol in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, and her rise and fall have inspired historians, artists and novelists.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zenobia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Egypt was lost to Palmyra under <b>Zenobia</b> around 270 and recovered by Aurelian, and Diocletian besieged Alexandria to crush a usurpation in 297–298 and withdrew the southern frontier to Philae in the face of the Blemmyes and Nobades.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-egypt">Roman Egypt · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 270 <b>Zenobia</b>, ruling Palmyra for her young son Vaballathus, sent her general Zabdas into Egypt, helped by the local supporter Timagenes.</blockquote><a href="/state/palmyrene-egypt">Palmyrene Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote>His murder in 267 left power to his widow <b>Zenobia</b>, regent for their young son Vaballathus.</blockquote><a href="/state/palmyrene-empire">Palmyrene Empire</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palmyra">Palmyra</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.56,38.27&amp;year=262" aria-label="Palmyra on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5522 912 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Zenobia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5972 1182h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5972 1182h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.56,38.27&amp;year=262">What was here in 262 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
